## What this cluster does and does not give you

| capability | status | why |
|---|---|---|
| Single management UI and API for three nodes | **yes** | that is what clustering provides |
| Central user/permission and firewall configuration | **yes** | `/etc/pve` is replicated by pmxcfs |
| Cold/offline migration between nodes | **yes** | over the cluster network |
| Live migration between nodes | **only with shared storage** | guest disks live on node-local LVM/ZFS |
| Automatic failover (HA) | **NO** | HA is deliberately not enabled: there is no shared guest storage, so HA would fence nodes without being able to restart the guests |
| Backup redundancy | **NO** | the Synology is a single backup target and a single point of failure |

### Explicit warnings

1. **Clustering is not high availability.** Three nodes give quorum and one UI.
   Nothing restarts a guest automatically when a node dies.
2. **Local VM storage is not shared storage.** `local-lvm` on one node is invisible
   to the others. Live migration and HA both require shared or replicated storage
   (Ceph, ZFS replication, or an NFS/iSCSI datastore for guest disks).
3. **Backup-only NFS provides no failover.** The Synology holds archives, not
   running disks. If a node dies, recovery means "restore from backup", measured
   in hours.
4. **Rebuild security labs in isolation.** Metasploitable is not restored by this
   migration. If recreated later, it must never be attached to a routed bridge.
5. **The Synology is one dependency.** One NAS, one volume, one building. It is not
   a 3-2-1 backup strategy — see the recommendation below.

## Recommended next steps

1. **Configure a scheduled backup job** on the cluster (Datacenter → Backup) writing
   to `{{ nas_storage_id }}`. The migration deliberately leaves scheduling to you so
   that no automatic job starts mid-migration.
2. **Move toward 3-2-1**: three copies, two media types, one off-site. Today you have
   one copy on one NAS.
3. **Deploy Proxmox Backup Server.** PBS gives deduplication, incremental backups,
   server-side verification jobs and remote sync to a second PBS — all of which this
   NFS target cannot do. Point PBS's datastore at Volume 1 and sync it off-site.
4. **Revisit Storage Pool 1.** It reports a health warning and Volume 2 has 0 bytes
   free. That is a failure waiting to happen next to your only backup copy.
5. **Decide about shared storage** if live migration or HA ever matters.
